# proc/refused.poke -- the ways spawn says no, and the one way it says yes to
# something that then fails.
#
# The interesting case is the last kind: spawning a program that does not
# exist SUCCEEDS. fork worked, the child ran, execve failed, and the child
# exited 127. That is POSIX, it is what every shell reports, and a broker that
# tried to hide it would have to wait for the child before answering -- which
# would make every spawn synchronous to make one error tidier.
#
# Frames transcribed by hand from ABI.md sections 4 and 7.14 to 7.16.
#
#   hello  op 01, len 10, with one preopen: 68 bytes back
EMIT 01 0a 00 42 53 54 4d 01 00 00 00 00 00
READ 68
#   pipe with an undefined flag bit -- INVAL 06
EMIT 0e 02 00 01 00
READ 3
#   spawn with nargv 0 -- INVAL 06. A child with no argv[0] is not a thing
#   this ABI can express.
EMIT 0f 0f 00 01 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 03 00 62 66 69
READ 3
#   spawn with the path ".." -- DENIED 04, from the same checker the
#   filesystem ops use. spawn fchdirs to the directory handle and execs a
#   relative path, so ".." escapes exactly as it would in open.
EMIT 0f 12 00 01 00 00 00 00 00 00 01 00 00 02 00 2e 2e 02 00 2e 2e
READ 3
#   spawn with an absolute path -- INVAL 06. There are none in this ABI.
EMIT 0f 1c 00 01 00 00 00 00 00 00 01 00 00
EMIT 07 00 2f 62 69 6e 2f 73 68
EMIT 07 00 2f 62 69 6e 2f 73 68
READ 3
#   spawn "nope", which does not exist.  This SUCCEEDS: handle 2.
EMIT 0f 16 00 01 00 00 00 00 00 00 01 00 00
EMIT 04 00 6e 6f 70 65
EMIT 04 00 6e 6f 70 65
READ 7
#   wait for it -- state 1 exited, code 127, because the child could not exec
EMIT 10 06 00 02 00 00 00 00 00
READ 7
#   wait on the preopened directory, which is not a process -- BADF 03, from
#   the handle's kind
EMIT 10 06 00 01 00 00 00 00 00
READ 3
EMIT 0c 04 00 02 00 00 00
READ 3
#   exit  op 02, len 1, code 0
EMIT 02 01 00 00
READ 3
